.TH OTPME-PASS 1 "2026-10-22" "OTPme 0.3.0a286" "OTPme Manual"
.SH NAME
otpme-pass \- manage passwords
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B otpme-pass
.I command
[\fIoptions\fR] [\fIpassword\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.na
.nh
.B otpme-pass
keeps passwords in OTPme, for example the iDRAC and root passwords of a
server.
.PP
A password object holds one or more entries.
Each entry has a name, a username, a password, a URL and notes.
So the object \fBserver1\fR can hold the entries \fBidrac\fR and \fBroot\fR.
.PP
The entries are encrypted on the client, the host you work on.
The nodes only store what they cannot read.
Entry names, usernames, URLs, notes and passwords never leave the client
unencrypted.
What the nodes see in clear text is the name of the object, its unit, its description and info, the objects it belongs to, and which users hold a key, as well as its ACLs, policies, config parameters and changelog.
None of these name an entry.
.PP
Every password object has a key of its own.
That key is stored once for every user who may read the password,
encrypted with the encrypt public key of that user.
To read a password, the key script of the user decrypts this key, with
the private key of the user (see \fBkey_script\fR in
.BR otpme\-user (1)).
.PP
What opens or writes the entries runs on hosts only: \fBadd\fR with an entry, \fBchange_entry\fR, \fBdel_entry\fR, \fBshow_password\fR, \fBlist_entries\fR, \fBupdate_keys\fR, \fB\-\-offline\fR, and \fBshow_password\fR of \fBotpme\-node\fR and the others.
On a node they refuse: a node holds the tree, and the passwords would be in clear there.
The other commands work on a node as well.
.PP
A name alone puts a new password into the unit a \fBdefaultunits\fR policy names, see
.BR otpme\-policy (1),
else into \fBpasswords\fR.
A path such as \fBpasswords/servers/server1\fR puts it into that unit.
Any unit will do; \fBpasswords\fR is only where they go by default.
Password objects are synced to the nodes of their own site only, never to hosts and never to other sites.
Nor is the unit \fBpasswords\fR with everything below it; another unit with passwords in it is synced, the passwords are not.
What a host keeps, \fBotpme\-agent\fR fetches, see \fBOffline use\fR.
.SS Who gets a key
The config parameter
.B password_store_members
of a unit or site names the members: tokens and roles.
A token stands for its owner.
A role stands for the owners of its tokens, and of the tokens of the
roles in it.
The nearest unit that sets the parameter counts, else the site.
.PP
The members must be signed.
A user signs them with
.B otpme\-unit sign_password_members
(or
.BR otpme\-site ).
The signature covers the members and the users they stand for at that
moment.
.PP
The client checks the signatures, not the nodes.
It trusts only signers of type \fBpassword\fR that are set up on the host:
.RS
.B otpme\-tool add_signer \-\-signer\-type password \(aquser|example.org/alice\(aq
.RE
.PP
Without such a signer, or without a valid signature, nothing is
encrypted.
So whoever runs a node cannot add themselves to the members.
.PP
A key goes only to users that a valid signature names and that are still
members.
When the members change, the signature is out of date:
.IP \(bu 2
A user who joins gets no key until the members are signed again.
.IP \(bu 2
A user who leaves loses the key with the next \fBupdate_keys\fR.
.PP
A user without an encrypt public key gets no key.
OTPme says so.
.PP
With \fBPIN_USER_KEYS\fR in
.BR otpme.conf (5),
the host also checks the encrypt public key of each member against the one
written down on the host.
.SS Who may do what
ACLs decide what a user may do with a password object.
The key decides whether the user can read it.
Both are needed.
.PP
.B view:entries
allows \fBshow_password\fR and \fBlist_entries\fR.
\fBadd:entry\fR, \fBedit:entry
and
.B delete:entry
allow changes to entries.
.B edit:keys
allows \fBupdate_keys\fR.
Tab completion after \fBadd_acl\fR offers all ACLs of a password object.
.PP
The unit \fBpasswords\fR gets no ACLs from its site.
Give them by hand.
Recursive ACLs of the site do not reach password objects either.
Who may see a password is said on its unit, and on nothing above it.
The usual ACLs are in the base ACL group \fBPASSUSER\fR, see \fBACL Management\fR.
.SS Passwords of other objects
A password can belong to nodes, hosts, devices and clients, for example
the iDRAC password of a node.
Add it with \fB\-\-node\fR and the others, or later with \fBadd_node\fR and
the others.
.PP
Then
.B otpme\-node show_password
\fInode\fR [\fIentry\fR] shows the passwords of that node, and the same
for hosts, devices and clients.
Each password decides with its own \fBview:entries\fR whether it is shown.
.PP
When such an object is deleted, its passwords stay.
The delete question says how many there are.
.SS Offline use
A host can keep passwords, so that they can be read when no node can be
reached.
.PP
The config parameter
.B password_offline_cache
of a unit or site names the hosts that may keep the passwords below it.
It takes hosts, groups and roles of the site, as OIDs:
.PP
.RS
.nf
.B host|example.org/host1
.B group|example.org/admins
.B role|example.org/berlin/servers
.fi
.RE
.PP
A name without a type is a host.
A group or role stands for the hosts in it, and the hosts of its roles.
One that is disabled stands for no host.
.PP
The nearest unit that sets it counts, else the site.
An empty value on a unit keeps its passwords off every host:
.PP
.RS
.B otpme\-unit config passwords/servers password_offline_cache \(dq\(dq
.RE
.PP
Removing the last entry with \fB\-d\fR leaves the value empty as well.
\fB\-d\fR without a value removes the parameter, and the unit above counts again.
Without the parameter anywhere, no host keeps anything.
.PP
.B otpme\-agent
fetches the passwords for the user who is logged in: at login, every
\fBAGENT_PASS_CACHE_INTERVAL\fR seconds (see
.BR otpme.conf (5)),
and when a password or \fBpassword_offline_cache\fR changes.
A host that joins or leaves a group or role is seen at the next interval.
A host keeps only what the user may read: the passwords with
\fBview:entries\fR and a key for that user.
It keeps them as they are stored, encrypted.
The file stays after logout.
.PP
A user whose private key is kept on the server (\fBkey_mode server\fR)
gets nothing to keep.
Reading needs the key script on the host, and the login leaves it there.
.PP
When no node can be reached, \fBshow\fR, \fBlist\fR, \fBlist_entries\fR
and \fBshow_password\fR read what the host keeps.
With \fB\-\-offline\fR they read it without asking a node.
Both say on stderr how old the kept passwords are.
Nothing can be changed offline.
.PP
Offline, \fBshow\fR has fewer columns: Password, Unit, Nodes, Hosts,
Devices, Clients and Description.
.hy
.ad
.SH COMMANDS
.SS Password Management
.na
.nh
.TP
\fBadd\fR [\fB\-\-node\fR \fInode\fR] [\fB\-\-host\fR \fIhost\fR] [\fB\-\-device\fR \fIdevice\fR] [\fB\-\-client\fR \fIclient\fR] [\fB\-\-username\fR \fIusername\fR] [\fB\-\-url\fR \fIurl\fR] [\fB\-\-notes\fR \fInotes\fR] \fIpassword\fR [\fIentry\fR]
Add a password object.
With a path, e.g. \fBpasswords/servers/server1\fR, it goes into that unit; else into the unit a \fBdefaultunits\fR policy names, see
.BR otpme\-policy (1),
or into \fBpasswords\fR.
The name is unique in the site, whatever unit it is in, and is taken in lower case.
It may be \fBmax_pass_name_len\fR long (64 by default).
.br
With \fIentry\fR the first entry is added as well: the password is asked for twice, and \fB\-\-username\fR, \fB\-\-url\fR and \fB\-\-notes\fR fill the other fields, see \fBEntries\fR.
\fB\-\-node\fR, \fB\-\-host\fR, \fB\-\-device\fR and \fB\-\-client\fR name an object the password belongs to, see \fBPasswords of other objects\fR above.
.br
When the password object is there already, \fBadd\fR adds the \fIentry\fR to it, and needs \fBadd:entry\fR; without an \fIentry\fR it says "Password already exists".
\fB\-\-node\fR and the others are refused then: use \fBadd_node\fR and the others, see \fBObjects\fR.
.br
Needs \fBadd:pass\fR on the unit.
.TP
\fBdel\fR [\fB\-\-no\-trash\fR] \fIpassword\fR
Delete the password object.
It goes to the trash, see
.BR otpme\-trash (1);
\fB\-\-no\-trash\fR deletes it right away.
The hosts that keep it are told, and drop it.
.br
Needs \fBdelete:object\fR on the password or \fBdelete:pass\fR on the unit.
.TP
\fBshow\fR [\fB\-\-offline\fR] [\fB\-a\fR] [\fB\-z\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-\-fields\fR \fIF1,F2\fR] [\fB\-\-limit\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-\-policy\-limit\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-\-sort\-by\fR \fIfield\fR] [\fB\-\-reverse\fR] [\fB\-\-raw\fR] [\fB\-\-csv\fR] [\fB\-\-csv\-sep\fR \fIsep\fR] [\fIpassword\fR]
Without a password: a table of all of them, with their unit, the nodes, hosts, devices and clients they belong to, policies, ACL inheritance and description.
The entries are not in it, see \fBshow_password\fR.
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-offline
The passwords kept on this host, without asking a node, see \fBOffline use\fR above.
.TP
.B \-a
Also the passwords you may only look at, not just the ones you may change.
.TP
.B \-z \fIN\fR
How many passwords to print.
.TP
.B \-\-fields \fIF1,F2\fR
Print these columns and no others, in the order they are named.
A name with a \fB+\fR in front is added to the columns that are shown anyway, and one with a \fB\-\fR is left out of them.
.TP
.B \-\-limit \fIN\fR
How many of each kind to print per password: nodes, hosts, devices, clients, policies.
.TP
.B \-\-policy\-limit \fIN\fR
The same for the policies only.
.TP
.B \-\-sort\-by \fIfield\fR, \fB\-\-reverse\fR
Which column to sort by, and the other way round.
.TP
.B \-\-raw\fR, \fB\-\-csv\fR, \fB\-\-csv\-sep\fR \fIsep\fR
\fB\-\-raw\fR leaves the header and the borders out, \fB\-\-csv\fR prints one row per line with \fB;\fR between the columns, and \fB\-\-csv\-sep\fR uses another separator.
.RE
.IP
A column you may not see says \fB\-\fR.
With a password named, this prints the object itself instead of a table row, one value per line, e.g. \fBNODES\fR; that needs \fBview_public:object\fR.
\fBshow\fR \f(BIpassword\fB$\fR prints that one password as a table row.
A name that is no password is a pattern with \fB*\fR, not a regex, and a unit path that ends with \fB/\fR shows the passwords of that unit, see
.B SELECTING OBJECTS
in
.BR otpme (1).
.TP
\fBshow_oid\fR [\fB\-\-full\fR] \fIpassword\fR
Show the OID of the password: \fB\%pass|<realm>/<site>/<name>\fR.
With \fB\-\-full\fR the full OID, which names the unit as well: \fB\%pass|<realm>/<site>/<unit_path>/<name>\fR.
.br
Needs the ACL \fBview_public:object\fR.
.TP
.B show_uuid \fIpassword\fR
Show the UUID of the password.
.br
Needs the ACL \fBview_public:object\fR.
.TP
\fBlist\fR [\fB\-\-offline\fR] [\fB\-a\fR] [\fB\-\-attribute\fR \fIattribute\fR] [\fIpattern\fR]
List the password names, and with a pattern only those that match it, e.g. \fBserver*\fR.
You get the passwords you may change; \fB\-a\fR adds those you may only look at.
\fB\-\-attribute\fR matches the pattern against that attribute instead of the name.
With \fB\-\-offline\fR the passwords kept on this host are searched the same way.
.TP
.B rename \fIpassword\fR \fInew_name\fR
Rename the password object.
The new name must not be in use in the site, and is taken in lower case.
.br
Needs \fBrename:object\fR.
.TP
\fBmove\fR [\fB\-\-keep\-acls\fR] \fIpassword\fR \fIunit\fR
Move the password object into another unit.
ACL inheritance is on for passwords, see \fBenable_acl_inheritance\fR: the default ACLs of the old unit are taken away and those of the new one given.
\fB\-\-keep\-acls\fR keeps all ACLs as they are.
.br
The members of the new unit count from then on, see \fBWho gets a key\fR above.
Run \fBupdate_keys\fR when they differ.
.br
Needs \fBmove\fR on the password and \fBadd:pass\fR on the unit.
.TP
.B touch \fIpassword\fR
Write the index entries of the password again; the hosts that keep it fetch it anew.
.br
Needs \fBtouch\fR.
.hy
.ad
.SS Entries
.na
.nh
An entry is added with \fBadd\fR, see above.
Only the client sees the entries: it opens them with the key of the user, see DESCRIPTION.
.PP
A change writes all entries again.
So it needs a key as well, like reading, unless the password has no entries yet, and members with a valid signature, see \fBWho gets a key\fR above.
Members that have no key yet get one with the change; one who left keeps it until \fBupdate_keys\fR.
.TP
\fBshow_password\fR [\fB\-\-offline\fR] \fIpassword\fR [\fIentry\fR]
Print the entries with their passwords, or only \fIentry\fR:
.RS
.PP
.nf
server1/idrac
    username: root
    password: ...
    url: https://idrac.example.org
    notes:
.fi
.RE
.IP
\fB\-\-offline\fR reads what this host keeps, see \fBOffline use\fR above.
Online, each call is written to the audit log.
.br
Needs \fBview:entries\fR and a key.
.TP
\fBlist_entries\fR [\fB\-\-offline\fR] \fIpassword\fR
The same without the passwords.
.br
Needs \fBview:entries\fR and a key.
.TP
\fBchange_entry\fR [\fB\-\-username\fR \fIusername\fR] [\fB\-\-url\fR \fIurl\fR] [\fB\-\-notes\fR \fInotes\fR] [\fB\-\-password\fR] \fIpassword\fR \fIentry\fR
Change the fields given; an empty value, e.g. \fB\-\-notes ""\fR, clears one.
\fB\-\-password\fR asks for a new password, twice.
Without any of them, nothing is changed and it says so.
.br
Needs \fBedit:entry\fR and a key.
.TP
.B del_entry \fIpassword\fR \fIentry\fR
Delete the entry.
.br
Needs \fBdelete:entry\fR and a key.
.hy
.ad
.SS Keys
.na
.nh
.TP
\fBupdate_keys \f(BIpassword\fR | \fB\-\-all\fR [\fB\-\-unit\fR \fIunit\fR]
Bring the keys of the password in line with its members, see \fBWho gets a key\fR above: members that have no key get one, and users that are no longer members lose it.
Run it after the members were signed again, after a user left, and after \fBmove\fR.
.IP
When a user lost the key, the client makes a new key and encrypts all entries with it, so a key kept from before opens nothing written from then on.
What that user read before, they may still know: change those passwords.
.IP
\fB\-\-all\fR does every password of this site you may give keys of, one by one; the others are left out.
\fB\-\-unit\fR limits that to one unit and the units below it.
At the end it says how many were done, and names the ones that failed.
\fB\-\-all\fR and \fB\-\-unit\fR are refused together with a \fIpassword\fR.
.br
Needs \fBedit:keys\fR, and a key yourself, unless the password has no keys yet.
.hy
.ad
.SS Objects
.na
.nh
Which nodes, hosts, devices and clients a password belongs to, see \fBPasswords of other objects\fR above.
Nothing is needed on the object itself, only the ACL on the password.
When the object is deleted, the password keeps its UUID until \fBremove_orphans\fR.
.TP
.B add_node \fIpassword\fR \fInode\fR
.TQ
.B remove_node \fIpassword\fR \fInode\fR
The password belongs to this node of the site, or no longer.
.br
Needs \fBadd:node\fR or \fBremove:node\fR.
.TP
.B add_host \fIpassword\fR \fIhost\fR
.TQ
.B remove_host \fIpassword\fR \fIhost\fR
The password belongs to this host, of any site of the realm, or no longer.
\fBremove_host\fR also takes the UUID of a host that is gone.
.br
Needs \fBadd:host\fR or \fBremove:host\fR.
.TP
.B add_device \fIpassword\fR \fIdevice\fR
.TQ
.B remove_device \fIpassword\fR \fIdevice\fR
The password belongs to this device of the realm, or no longer.
.br
Needs \fBadd:device\fR or \fBremove:device\fR.
.TP
.B add_client \fIpassword\fR \fIclient\fR
.TQ
.B remove_client \fIpassword\fR \fIclient\fR
The password belongs to this client of the site, or no longer.
.br
Needs \fBadd:client\fR or \fBremove:client\fR.
.hy
.ad
.SS Object Changelog
.na
.nh
Every change to the object writes an entry: who made it, when, and what was done.
A change can carry a comment of its own, through the global option \fB\-\-changelog\fR.
.PP
For the entries of a password it says "add entry", "change entry" or "delete entry", never which one: the name stays on the client, like the rest of the entry.
Reading them is not a change; \fBshow_password\fR goes to the audit log instead.
.PP
The \fBchangelog\fR config parameter can switch that off.
It is read along the cascade of the password, and nothing is written where it is off \(em not for that password, and not for the ones below a unit that switched it off.
Without any value anywhere it is on.
.PP
An \fIentry_id\fR is the number the listing shows in front of an entry, counted from 0.
.PP
Only the comment of an entry can be changed or removed.
What was done and by whom stays as it is.
.PP
A command that changes something asks first; the global option \fB\-f\fR skips the question.
.TP
.B changelog \fIpassword\fR
Show the changelog of the password object, one entry per line: its \fIentry_id\fR, the time, who made the change and what was done, then a detail in round brackets and the comment in square brackets where there are any.
.br
Needs the ACL \fBview:changelog\fR.
.TP
.B edit_changelog \fIpassword\fR \fIentry_id\fR \fIcomment\fR
Set the comment of that entry, or replace the one it has.
That writes an entry of its own.
.br
Needs the ACL \fBedit:changelog\fR.
.TP
.B del_changelog \fIpassword\fR \fIentry_id\fR
Remove the comment of that entry.
The entry itself stays, and an entry without a comment is refused.
That writes an entry of its own.
.br
Needs the ACL \fBedit:changelog\fR.
.TP
.B clear_changelog \fIpassword\fR
Remove every entry of the changelog.
Afterwards it holds one entry: that it was cleared.
.br
This needs more than the ACL \fBclear:changelog\fR: the config parameter
.B allow_changelog_clearing
has to allow it (see \fBotpme\fR(7)).
It is true by default, but where no value is found at all, clearing is refused.
A value set on the token you are signed in with counts first.
Without one, the value of the object counts, read along its cascade.
.hy
.ad
.SS Policy Management
.na
.nh
A \fIpolicy\fR is the name of a policy, not a path.
It is looked for in the site of the password.
Which policies there are, and what each of them does, is in
.BR otpme\-policy (1).
.PP
One type does something on a password:
.B authonaction
asks whoever changes the password to authenticate for it again, see \fBAUTH ON ACTION HOOKS\fR below.
Every other type is refused.
.PP
Reading the entries has no hook: \fB\-\-offline\fR reads what the host keeps without asking a node, so a policy could not stop it.
What guards the entries is the key of the user, e.g. on a YubiKey.
.PP
Attaching and detaching ask before they change anything; the global option \fB\-f\fR skips the question.
.TP
.B add_policy \fIpassword\fR \fIpolicy\fR
Attach a policy to the password object.
.br
A policy has a list of object types it is for; one that does not name \fBpass\fR is refused.
So is a second policy of a type that allows only one per object.
.br
Needs the ACL \fBassign:policy\fR.
.TP
.B remove_policy \fIpassword\fR \fIpolicy\fR
Remove a policy from the password object.
.br
Needs the ACL \fBremove:policy\fR, and that one verbatim: a plain \fBremove\fR does not cover it.
Detaching a policy takes away what it enforces, so it must not be cheaper than attaching one.
.TP
\fBlist_policies\fR [\fB\-\-return\-type\fR \fITYPE\fR] [\fB\-\-policy\-types\fR \fIt1,t2\fR] \fIpassword\fR
List the policies attached to the password object, the disabled ones too, marked \fB(disabled)\fR.
Use \fB\-\-return\-type\fR to select the attribute returned (\fBname\fR, \fBread_oid\fR, \fBfull_oid\fR, \fBuuid\fR).
Use \fB\-\-policy\-types\fR to list only policies of the given types; these are policy types, not policy names.
.br
Needs the ACL \fBview:policies\fR.
.hy
.ad
.SS ACL Management
.na
.nh
An ACL says what somebody may do with this password object.
The somebody is a role or a token, never a user.
.PP
What the three forms \fBacl\fR, \fB+acl\fR and \fB++acl\fR mean is under \fBACLs\fR in
.BR otpme (7).
Tab completion after \fBadd_acl\fR offers all ACLs of a password object.
.PP
The usual ACLs for working with the passwords of a unit are in the base ACL group \fBPASSUSER\fR, see
.BR otpme\-aclgroup (1).
Give it on the unit as \fB@PASSUSER,+@PASSUSER\fR: \fB@PASSUSER\fR lets the owner add and delete passwords in the unit, \fB+@PASSUSER\fR work with the passwords in it.
\fB++@PASSUSER\fR reaches the passwords in the sub units as well.
Moving, policies and ACLs are not in it.
.PP
A command that changes something asks first; the global option \fB\-f\fR skips the question.
.TP
.B add_acl \fIpassword\fR \fIowner_type\fR \fIowner\fR \fIacl\fR[:\fIvalue\fR][,\fIacl\fR[:\fIvalue\fR]]
Give a role or a token one or more ACLs on this password object.
.br
\fIowner_type\fR is \fBrole\fR or \fBtoken\fR.
\fIowner\fR names it the way \fBlist_acls\fR prints it: a role as \fIsite/name\fR, or by its name alone on its own site; a token as \fI\%user/token\fR.
An ACL can carry a value, for example \fBview:entries\fR.
To give several ACLs at once, separate them with commas: \fB\%view:entries,edit:entry\fR.
.br
Needs the ACL \fBadd:acl\fR.
.TP
.B del_acl \fIpassword\fR \fIacl\fR[,\fIacl\fR]
Take those ACLs away again.
.br
Here an \fIacl\fR is the whole entry, owner and all, the way \fBlist_acls\fR prints it: \fBrole:berlin/helpdesk:view:entries\fR.
.br
Needs the ACL \fBdelete:acl\fR.
.TP
.B list_acls \fIpassword\fR
Show the ACLs that lie on this password object, i.e. who may do what with it.
.br
Needs the ACL \fBview:acl\fR.
.TP
.B enable_acl_inheritance \fIpassword\fR
Let a move of the password swap its default ACLs: what the old unit handed down is taken away, and what the new unit hands down is given.
It does by default, because who may see a password is said on its unit.
.RS
.PP
Only a move asks this.
A new password gets the default ACLs of its unit, and \fBadd_acl \-a\fR or \fB\-r\fR on a unit reach it either way.
A move with \fB\-\-keep\-acls\fR leaves its ACLs alone all the same.
.PP
A move by somebody who does not hold \fBadd:acl\fR and \fBdelete:acl\fR on the password leaves its ACLs alone as well, and says so.
Otherwise moving a password into a unit would be a way of handing yourself the default ACLs of that unit.
.PP
Needs the ACL \fBenable:acl_inheritance\fR.
.RE
.TP
.B disable_acl_inheritance \fIpassword\fR
A move leaves the ACLs of the password as they are: whoever could reach it in the old unit still can, and the new unit gives nobody anything.
.br
Needs the ACL \fBdisable:acl_inheritance\fR.
.hy
.ad
.SS Configuration
.na
.nh
\fBconfig\fR sets the parameters every object has; for a password these are \fBchangelog\fR and \fBallow_changelog_clearing\fR, see \fBObject Changelog\fR.
.PP
A command that changes something asks first; the global option \fB\-f\fR skips the question.
.TP
\fBconfig\fR [\fB\-a\fR] [\fB\-d\fR] \fIpassword\fR \fIparameter\fR \fR[\fIvalue\fR]
Set a parameter on the password object.
.RS
.TP
.B \-d
Remove the parameter from the password object, or with a value only that one value from a list.
It is not set back to a default: what applies afterwards is whatever the unit or the site says.
.TP
.B \-a
Append the value to a parameter that holds a list, instead of replacing what is there.
.RE
.IP
The ACL is per parameter, \fBset:config:<parameter>\fR.
.TP
.B get_config \fIpassword\fR \fIparameter\fR
What the parameter comes to for this password object, once the object, its units and its site have been looked at.
So this answers what applies, where \fBshow_config\fR answers what was set here.
.br
Needs the ACL \fBview:config:<parameter>\fR.
.TP
.B show_config \fIpassword\fR [\fIparameter\fR]
The parameters set on the password object itself, or only the one named.
Nothing is shown for a parameter that it gets from a unit or from the site.
.br
Needs the ACL \fBview:config:<parameter>\fR; without it a parameter is left out of the list, and one named is refused.
.TP
.B description \fIpassword\fR \fR[\fIdescription\fR]
Set the description.
Without one OTPme asks and offers the current text for editing.
It is not encrypted: \fBshow\fR lists it, and a host keeps it for \fB\-\-offline\fR.
.br
Needs the ACL \fBedit:description\fR.
.TP
\fBinfo\fR [\fB\-\-language\fR \fILANG\fR] \fIpassword\fR \fR[\fIinfo\fR]
Set free-form info text.
It is not encrypted.
If \fIinfo\fR is omitted, the current info text is opened in the editor named by the \fBEDITOR\fR environment variable (default: vim).
.br
Needs the ACL \fBedit:info\fR.
.br
Without
.B \-\-language
the text is the administrator's own note.
Nothing shows the info of a password to a user, so a text for a language stays a note as well.
.TP
\fBdump_info\fR [\fB\-\-language\fR \fILANG\fR] \fIpassword\fR
Write one of those texts to stdout.
\fB\-\-language\fR picks which one; without it you get the note of the administrator.
.br
Needs the ACL \fBview:info\fR.
.hy
.ad
.SS Export and Cleanup
.na
.nh
There is no import on this page: reading an export back in is \fBotpme\-tool import\fR, see
.BR otpme\-tool (1).
.PP
.B remove_orphans
asks before it changes anything; the global option \fB\-f\fR skips the question.
.TP
\fBexport\fR [\fB\-\-password\fR \fIpassphrase\fR] \fIpassword\fR
Write the whole password object to stdout, as JSON.
.RS
.PP
Without \fB\-\-password\fR it is written as it is, in clear.
With one, a key is derived from that passphrase and the object is encrypted with it; the salt goes into the output, so the passphrase is all you need to read it back.
.PP
The entries stay encrypted either way: only a user with a key of the password can read them, as with \fBshow_password\fR.
.PP
The nodes, hosts, devices and clients of the password and the users with a key are named by UUID, so an export says something only in the realm it came from.
.PP
Needs the ACL \fBexport\fR.
.RE
.TP
.B remove_orphans \fIpassword\fR
Remove what the password object still points at although it is gone.
.RS
.PP
That is its ACLs and its policies, the nodes, hosts, devices and clients it belongs to, and the keys of users that are gone.
.PP
It shows everything it found and asks once for all of it.
It asks nothing and changes nothing when it finds nothing.
.PP
Needs the ACL \fBremove:orphans\fR.
.RE
.hy
.ad
.SH OPTIONS
Every option belongs to one command and is described with that command above.
.PP
Global options work with every command, see
.BR otpme (1).
.SH EXAMPLES
.na
.nh
.SS Who gets the keys
.TP
.B otpme-unit config passwords password_store_members \(aqrole|example.org/berlin/admins\(aq
The users of the role \fBadmins\fR get the keys of the passwords in the unit \fBpasswords\fR.
.TP
.B otpme-unit sign_password_members passwords
A user signs the members.
Until then nobody gets a key.
.TP
.B otpme-tool add_signer \-\-signer\-type password \(aquser|example.org/alice\(aq
On each host, as root: trust the signatures of \fBalice\fR.
.SS A password for a node
.TP
.B otpme-pass add \-\-node node1 \-\-username root \-\-url https://node1\-idrac.example.org node1 idrac
The password \fBnode1\fR with its first entry \fBidrac\fR, for the node \fBnode1\fR.
The password of the entry is asked for twice.
.TP
.B otpme-pass show_password node1 idrac
The entry \fBidrac\fR with its password.
.TP
.B otpme-node show_password node1
All passwords of the node \fBnode1\fR.
.TP
.B otpme-pass change_entry \-\-password node1 idrac
A new password for the entry.
.SS Passwords in a unit of their own
.TP
.B otpme-unit add passwords/servers
.TQ
.B otpme-pass add passwords/servers/server1 root
The unit \fBpasswords/servers\fR, and the password \fBserver1\fR in it with the entry \fBroot\fR.
.TP
.B otpme-pass update_keys \-\-all \-\-unit passwords/servers
Bring the keys of all passwords below \fBpasswords/servers\fR in line with the members, e.g. after a user has left.
.SS Offline use
.TP
.B otpme-unit config passwords password_offline_cache \(aqhost|example.org/host1,host|example.org/host2\(aq
The hosts \fBhost1\fR and \fBhost2\fR keep the passwords of the unit \fBpasswords\fR for offline use.
.TP
.B otpme-unit config \-a passwords password_offline_cache \(aqgroup|example.org/admins\(aq
The hosts in the group \fBadmins\fR keep them as well.
.TP
.B otpme-pass show_password \-\-offline node1
Read the password \fBnode1\fR from what this host keeps, without asking a node.
.hy
.ad
.\" BEGIN AUTH ON ACTION HOOKS
.\" Generated by update_hooks_man.py. Do not edit, the next run
.\" overwrites it.
.SH AUTH ON ACTION HOOKS
An auth on action policy can ask for a login again before these actions.
See
.B Auth On Action Policy
in
.BR otpme\-policy (1).
A pass has the hooks every object type has, and these; a command of another name is in brackets:
.RS
.PP
.na
.nh
add_client, add_device, add_entry\ (add), add_host, add_node, change_entry, del_entry, remove_client, remove_device, remove_host, remove_node, update_keys.
.hy
.ad
.RE
.\" END AUTH ON ACTION HOOKS
.SH FILES
.na
.nh
.TP
\fI/var/lib/otpme/tree/\fR
The objects of the realm.
A password is the directory \fI<name>.pass\fR in the directory of its unit, e.g. \fI<realm>.realm/<site>.site/passwords.unit/node1.pass/\fR, with its settings in \fIobject.json\fR, on the nodes of its site only, never on a host.
Change them with the commands above, not in the files.
.TP
\fI/var/cache/otpme/offline/<user_uuid>/pass.json\fR
The passwords a host keeps for the user, see \fBOffline use\fR.
The directory is \fIoffline\fR below \fBCACHE_DIR\fR in
.BR otpme.conf (5).
\fBotpme\-agent\fR writes it for the user, readable by the user alone, and replaces it as a whole on every fetch.
The entries in it stay encrypted; the names, units, descriptions and the objects the passwords belong to are in clear.
.hy
.ad
